1. what does public vistual destructors does ? 

virtual destructors ensures that a derived cass object is deeted through a pointer or reference to the base calss, both the derived and base cass destructors execute in the proper order. 

2. why it matters
   1. polymorphic deletion: when you deete a derived object using a pointer typed as base cass. c++ needs to now which destructor to run first. it ensures that the destructors of both the derived and base classes run in the correct order. 
   2. we often use a base class pointer or reference to manage a derived class object (polymorphism)
   3. Without a virtual destructor, if we delete a derived object using a pointer typed as the base class, c++ only calls the base class destructor. any memory or resources allocated inside the derived class are skipped, causing a memory leak. 
   4. with a virtual destructor, the program checks the actual type of the object at runtime and calls the derived class destructor first, followed by base class destructor. This guarantees complete and safe cleanup. 
3. what is the difference between abstract and virtual?
```
public abstract class Enemy 
{
    // ABSTRACT: Every enemy MUST attack, but they all do it completely differently.
    // There is no logical "default" attack.
    public abstract void Attack();

    // VIRTUAL: Most enemies walk normally, but a specific enemy (like a Ghost) 
    // might choose to fly instead. We provide a default way to move.
    public virtual void Move() 
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Walking forward on the ground.");
    }
}

